ractive and capacitive attributes. They can be integrated into electrical circuits with nonlinear electrical properties. Advancing fungal electronics","line":5},{"title":"we studied the AC conductive properties of mycelium-bound composites and fungal fruit bodies at higher frequencies across three overlapping bands; 20 Hz to 300 kHz","target":"we studied the AC conductive properties of mycelium-bound composites and fungal fruit bodies at higher frequencies across three overlapping bands; 20 Hz to 300 kHz","line":6},{"title":"10 Hz to 4 MHz and 50 kHz to 3 GHz. Measurements indicate that mycelium-bound composites typically act as low-pass filters with a mean cut-off frequency of ∼500 kHz; with ∼−14 dB/decade roll-off","target":"10 Hz to 4 MHz and 50 kHz to 3 GHz. Measurements indicate that mycelium-bound composites typically act as low-pass filters with a mean cut-off frequency of ∼500 kHz; with ∼−14 dB/decade roll-off","line":7},{"title":"and mean attenuation across the pass band of <<math><mo is=\\\"true\\\">&lt;</mo></math>1 dB. Fruiting bodies have between one or two orders of magnitude lower mean cut-off frequency (5 kHz–50 kHz depending on species); with −20 dB/decade to −30 dB/decade roll-off","target":"and mean attenuation across the pass band of <<math><mo is=\\\"true\\\">&lt;</mo></math>1 dB. Fruiting bodies have between one or two orders of magnitude lower mean cut-off frequency (5 kHz–50 kHz depending on species); with −20 dB/decade to −30 dB/decade roll-off","line":8},{"title":"and mean attenuation across the pass band of <<math><mo is=\\\"true\\\">&lt;</mo></math>3 dB. The mechanism for the frequency-dependent attenuation is uncertain; however","target":"and mean attenuation across the pass band of <<math><mo is=\\\"true\\\">&lt;</mo></math>3 dB. The mechanism for the frequency-dependent attenuation is uncertain; however","line":9},{"title":"the high water content","target":"the high water content","line":10},{"title":"which is electrically conductive due to dissolved ionisable solids is probably a key factor. The potential for mycelium-bound composites and fruiting bodies in analog computing is explored.","target":"which is electrically conductive due to dissolved ionisable solids is probably a key factor. The potential for mycelium-bound composites and fruiting bodies in analog computing is explored.","line":11},{"title":"Roshan Weerasekera a b","target":"Roshan Weerasekera a b","line":12},{"title":"Andrew Adamatzky a","target":"Andrew Adamatzky a","line":13}],"class_name":"SmartBlock"},
